    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the email-uniqueness rule in the owner create endpoint (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`). The V2 controller has no create method, so V1 is the sole `POST /api/owners` handler.\n\nThe check lives in the existing email-handling block, right after the email is validated and lower-cased:\n\n```java\nString emailKey = email.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);\nowner.setEmail(emailKey);\nboolean emailInUse = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()\n    .map(Owner::getEmail)\n    .filter(Objects::nonNull)\n    .map(existing -> existing.trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT))\n    .anyMatch(emailKey::equals);\nif (emailInUse) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n}\n```\n\nKey points:\n- Comparison is done on lower-cased (and trimmed) values on **both** sides, so a case-differing email counts as a duplicate regardless of how existing owners' emails were stored.\n- It only applies when an email is supplied (email is optional in this app), matching the existing null-guarded email handling.\n- Returns **409 Conflict**, consistent with the other conflict rules (telephone-in-use, city cap, household duplicate).\n\nThe new `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` acceptance test passes and the full build is green.",
